================ 77157f1
77157f1 P2-AR-0041: CIT state sealed on another machine is disclosed, not tampering (WS-2 x P2-ADJ-0002)

WS-2's round-3 D033 and os_binding_integrity CIT rows failed/graded high any non-verified CIT state
in force, including state sealed on another machine (FOREIGN/UNAUTHORISED/KEY_UNAVAILABLE); D033's
own rule and t2_severity disclose such records (not honoured here). cit::binding::binding_of now
carries the underlying t2 binding; sealed_elsewhere() lets D033 and the family apply the same rule:
modified/hand-written/copied state in force stays high. Found by ws08_r3's two-machine test
(machine C, anchor-only, must disclose the owner's records it does not honour).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>


 runtime/src/cit/binding.rs            | 17 ++++++++++++++++-
 runtime/src/doctor.rs                 |  5 ++++-
 runtime/src/verification/reporting.rs |  5 ++++-
 3 files changed, 24 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)
================ 8b3fd14
8b3fd14 P2-AR-0041: ws04r3 direct-propagation test observes the claim-time propagation (WS-4 x WS-5)

WS-5 wired WS-4 R2-3: task claim runs detect_and_propagate before establishing the claim, recording
the marks as WS-4's sealed system transaction. The test's premise (a change propagated after another
task's claim) can no longer arise through gov; it now asserts every property of the transaction the
claim records (origin system, COMMITTED, empty manifest, sealed, touched list, content-bound writes,
report re-sealed, obligation marked but not sealed), that the claiming task closes with its own work
only, and that a later gov cit propagate finds nothing left.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>


