================ 68f2e4a
68f2e4a P2-AR-0041: task records sealed on every OS write; task is a sealed kind (WS-5 r2 IP-R3-1, final step)

Ordered after WS-3's and WS-4's re-sealing of the task records they rewrite (both merged):
- every OS writer of a task record seals a record it creates and re-seals one it rewrites only when
  it verified before (the O-7 rule; tasks::save_task): tasks::{build_and_save (create,
  create_generated), set_status, set_status_internal (claim/release/continue), close,
  resolve_revalidation}, dag::replan, readiness::plan, generation::augment,
  verification::lineage::remediate (IP-R3-WS04-05); gates and cit::propagation already did;
- 'task' joins t2::SEALED_RECORD_TYPES: a hand-written or seal-stripped task record changed inside a
  claim window is refused at that close (MUTATION_SCOPE_VIOLATION, T2);
- the (sealed) claim baseline records task/CIT records already unsealed at claim time
  (tasks::LEGACY_SEALED_KINDS: legacy, or appended by a governed change); a gov operation that
  rewrites one of them inside the window cannot bless it, so the close reports it (os_managed_unbound)
  instead of refusing the worker — R3-WS5-8's purpose for records the OS never sealed;
- tasks::OS_MANAGED_PREFIXES gains governance/registry/ (IP-W7R3-1, required: a registration made
  inside a claim is recognised by its seal at close);
- adoption (WS-9 migrations::references): OS-generated work (verifying seal, OS-owned provenance:
  generation::is_os_generated) quotes the event it came from and is not a dependant — WS-2's new
  legacy_authority finding made WS-5 generate a remediation task naming the legacy file, which
  changed the destructive gate's subject and voided the owner's answer (brownfield batch 7);
- tests/certification/integration_r3.rs (new): the close-inside-another-claim-window proof, the
  forged/stripped/hand-created refusals, and the legacy-record report.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>


